+// n26: the blanket explode is the single largest destroyer of analytic geometry on real
+// mechanical parts, and the numbers say the escalation is wildly out of proportion to the
+// fault. Measured on mechparts/9 (46,104 tri, 88% curved):
+//
+//     N25_CHAINFAIL chains=11498 failed=69 regionsHit=129 recoverPass=0
+//     DIAG_EXPLODE x3777      cylRegions=98  smoothBuiltCylinders=0  shipped=0
+//
+// 69 of 11,498 chains fail -- 0.6%. The targeted arm explodes the 129 regions that own
+// them, the shell still does not close, and recoverPass 1 and 2 then call explodeAll(),
+// levelling all 3,777 analytic regions, 98 correctly built cylinders among them, to chase
+// 69 bad chains. Chain construction is 99.4% successful; the RESPONSE is the defect.
+//
+// So refuse the blanket when it would take out more than half the analytic cylinders --
+// the same 50% standard wouldSaturate() already applies to the U0/U1 cascade rungs, which
+// the J6 recovery loop never adopted. The build then keeps the faces it has; the shell may
+// stay open, and an open shell carrying 98 cylinders is a better answer than a closed one
+// carrying none. Env-gated; off => the original unconditional blanket.
